Title: NICK AND ME
Credit: Written by
Author: Adapted from Book
Source: Original Short Story
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

A worn fifth-floor apartment overlooking Milwaukee brick streets. PETER (30s, lean build, neat dark hair, wearing a sharp dark jacket) stands by the window holding an old photograph of two young boys.

(SOUND: Clock ticking steadily, distant city traffic humming outside)

PETER
(V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of. Steel wrecking balls for guts, a face like a puzzle held together with glue gun stuff.

Action: FLASHBACK - TEN YEARS EARLIER.

Younger PETER (20s, thin frame, plain t-shirt) opens the front door. NICK (20s, broad-shouldered hulk, messy reddish-brown hair, scars over his left eyebrow and chin, heavy work boots) marches in effortlessly hauling six bulging paper grocery bags.

(SOUND: Heavy boots stomping on floorboards, paper bags rustling)

MA (60s, frail frame, thin brown hair, tired eyes) sits in an overstuffed brown chair with a fleece afghan over her knees.

NICK
Got the heavy stuff, Ma. Little brother carried the receipt.

MA
(softly)
You're a good boy, Nick. What would I do without you two?

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Chalk dust floats past rusted weight benches. NICK, wearing a faded tank top showing scarred knuckles, approaches a bench press. A weightlifter immediately drops a 40-pound curl bar and backs away in fear.

(SOUND: Heavy iron weight plates clanging loudly on concrete)

NICK grabs a 50-pound curl bar with one hand, nods coldly, and begins warming up effortlessly. PETER stands in the background in plain shorts, holding a towel.

(MUSIC: Gritty low bass riff building)

PETER
(V.O.)
A fifty-pound bar just for warm-ups. I knew he'd stand up for me no matter what. He was our Viking.

EXT. PIZZERIA - DAY

Sunlight reflects off a cracked pavement sidewalk. SIONNA (20s, pale skin, short brown hair, striking light blue eyes, wearing a black V-neck, jeans, and a silver fish necklace) stumbles over a concrete edge and bumps into PETER (20s).

(SOUND: Bicycle bell ringing in street, footsteps scraping pavement)

PETER
Are you okay?

SIONNA
(brushing off her shirt)
Yes. I'm sorry.

PETER
I work here. I'm Peter. What's your name?

SIONNA
Sionna. Named after an Irish river goddess.

PETER
Sionna. That's different. Want to come in for a slice?

SIONNA
I don't eat pizza. But if you want a cup of coffee sometime, call me.

Sionna hands him a torn slip of paper with a phone number, turns, and walks away with a fluid, wave-like grace.

(SOUND: Wind rustling dry autumn leaves across pavement)

PETER
(V.O.)
She had the coolest eyes I ever saw. Light blue, like the sky right before sunrise.

INT. THE JAVA HUT - DAY

College students with textbooks scatter around wooden tables. SIONNA sits in a back booth wearing a loose green hooded top. PETER sits across from her, holding a paperback copy of Wuthering Heights.

(SOUND: Espresso machine steaming, background chatter of patrons)

SIONNA
Heathcliff's love for Catherine never changes, but in the end, that kind of unyielding love destroys him.

PETER
A love that never changes... destroys you?

SIONNA
If you don't let it flow where it's supposed to. We have to be willing to accept change without grasping onto things.

(MUSIC: Warm melancholic acoustic guitar notes)

PETER
(V.O.)
Something inside me was coming awake. She made me feel like a person by myself—without Nick, without Ma.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Cheap lamp light illuminates line-up medication bottles on the counter. MA sits in her chair, eyes empty and glassy. PETER steps in, setting down a library book.

(SOUND: Television game show blaring softly in background)

MA
Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

PETER
Ma, we haven't been to church in fifteen years. Mrs. Canasta moved down a floor last year.

The front door SLAMS open. NICK enters, boots heavy, face flushed red, hair wild.

(SOUND: Door slamming violently against wooden frame)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor appointment yesterday! Where the hell were you, Bro?

PETER
I forgot. I met a girl, Nick. We had a couple dates.

NICK
(clenching fists)
A girl? Try looking at your priorities! Holy Christ, do something before she turns into a demented old fart we can't afford!

(MUSIC: Tense low drone swells)

PETER
I said I was sorry!

NICK
(glaring, teeth bared)
Watch your mouth. You're living here to keep an eye on her. Don't test me.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

Soft candlelight flickers against purple rubber mats on polished wood floors. SIONNA lies flat on her mat in corpse pose. Beside her, PETER lies stretched out in shorts and a t-shirt, eyes closed, breathing slowly.

(SOUND: Soft singing bowl chime vibrating softly)

SIONNA
(whispering)
Your practice is your practice. Just let your mind settle into peace.

PETER
(quietly)
It's weird. I feel like... nobody's pushing me. Just calm.

(MUSIC: Gentle ethereal ambient hum)

PETER
(V.O.)
For the first time, I felt like I was growing up. I started taking college classes. I wanted Nick to be proud, but he only saw a threat.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Smoke hangs thick under neon beer signs. Pool balls crack on green felt tables. BOB (20s, scruffy, flannel shirt over a beer belly) blows cigarette smoke. NICK sits at the counter knocking back Jack and Coke. PETER sits quietly with a single beer bottle.

(SOUND: Heavy rock music blaring, pool balls clattering loud)

LINDSEY (20s, blonde) enters the pool room accompanied by a DARK-HAIRED MAN who puts a protective arm around her waist. NICK spots them and his face turns beet red.

(SOUND: Glass boots clinking, aggressive footsteps)

NICK
(rising)
Who the fuck is that asshole with Lindsey?

PETER
Nick, leave it alone. You broke up a year ago.

NICK
Kiss my ass!

NICK storms into the pool room, confronting the Dark-Haired Man.

DARK-HAIRED MAN
You got a problem, bitch?

NICK's eyes flare with rage. He draws back a heavy fist. PETER leaps between them, placing his hands on Nick's chest.

(SOUND: Crowd murmuring loudly, bar stools scraping)

PETER
Stop it! You're crazy as shit! No one cares about your ego, Nick!

NICK
(roaring)
Get out of my face or you're next!

NICK draws back his right fist and SWINGS wildly at Peter. Peter ducks backwards, dodging the blow by inches! JOE THE BAR OWNER and a BOUNCER tackle Nick against the pool table.

(SOUND: Heavy thud of bodies hitting wood, glasses shattering)

PETER turns and RUNS out the front door into the freezing night.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

A warm green suede sofa sits beside a tall bookshelf. PETER lies with his head in SIONNA's lap. She gently strokes his hair.

(SOUND: Rain tapping softly against windowpanes)

SIONNA
He tried to hit you because you're showing him you don't need him anymore. You're becoming your own person.

PETER
He's my brother. He took care of us when Dad left. I can't just throw him away.

SIONNA
You don't have to throw him away, Peter. But you can't let him stop your purpose.

(MUSIC: Tender string arrangement)

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

MA sits happily at the small table, eating a slice of warm pie topped with ice cream. SIONNA sits beside her, smiling. PETER works on a laptop computer nearby.

(SOUND: Silverware clinking gently on ceramic plates)

MA
On Halloween, my brothers would tip those outhouses right over! We were plain hoodlums!

The front door swings open. NICK steps in, wearing a suede jacket and heavy boots. He freezes, staring coldly at Sionna.

SIONNA
(standing, extending hand)
Hi Nick. I'm Sionna. Nice to meet you.

Nick looks at her hand like it's a poison snake, then shakes it reluctantly.

NICK
So, I hear you're the university professor telling my brother how to live.

SIONNA
(calm, poised)
I study philosophy and English. Diversity and education help people grow, Nick. Women bring important perspectives to the table.

NICK
(sneering)
Women going to school is why kids aren't raised right anymore.

SIONNA
Single parent families and economic reality mean women have to work. It's about contributing to society, not abandoning it.

Nick stands stunned, completely out-worded. He turns to Peter, grabbing Peter's collar in a tight squeeze.

(SOUND: Fabric tightening violently)

NICK
(whispering harshly)
Take care of Ma, nurse Nancy.

Nick slams the door violently as he leaves.

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL - ICU HALLWAY - DAY

Fluorescent lights buzz overhead. PETER stands looking through a glass window into an ICU room. Inside, NICK lies propped up in bed, leg in a traction rig, oxygen tube in his nose, face covered in deep yellow-purple bruises.

(SOUND: Heart monitor beeping rhythmically, hospital intercom paging)

OFFICER DUPUIS (50s, grey hair, police uniform) steps beside Peter.

OFFICER DUPUIS
His blood alcohol was twice the legal limit. He hit the intersection broadside. Your mother passed away at the scene, Mr. Olson. She didn't suffer.

Peter clutches the window ledge. Tears stream down his pale face.

(MUSIC: Dark heavy tragedy swell)

PETER
(voice shaking)
He killed her. He took her to church drunk... and he killed her.

OFFICER DUPUIS
He's facing involuntary manslaughter charges once he stabilizes. Do you want to step inside?

PETER
(turning away)
No. I never want to look at him again.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

A single lamp illuminates the dimly lit living room. SIONNA sits on the edge of her armchair, wearing loose cotton clothes. The front door is kicked unlocked.

NICK (limping heavily with a cane, face bruised, eyes wild) barges inside, smelling of stale liquor.

(SOUND: Door slamming hard, wooden cane striking floorboards)

SIONNA
(standing, alarmed)
Nick? How did you get in here?

NICK
You ruined my fucking life! You turned my brother against me, got Ma killed, and now the cops want ten years!

SIONNA
Nick, calm down—

NICK lunges forward, slamming Sionna against the hallway wall! He grabs her throat with his left hand.

(SOUND: Body slamming wooden wall, desperate gasp for air)

Nick pulls a Swiss Army knife from his pocket with his right hand and FLICKS the blade open!

(SOUND: Metallic blade CLICKing open sharply)

NICK
(hissing in her face)
I could kill you right now, you little whore! Break it off with him, or my friends will find your parents on their farm!

The front door BURSTS open! PETER rushes in, seeing the knife at Sionna's neck.

(SOUND: Door bursting open violently)

PETER
Nick! Get off her!

Peter grabs Nick's shoulder. Nick elbows Peter backward into the wall and SLASHES wildly with the knife! The blade CUTS into Sionna's upper shoulder!

(SOUND: SIONNA SCREAMS in pain, sharp slicing sound)

Sionna collapses to the floor, blood pooling on her shirt. NICK panics, drops his cane, and FLEES out the open front door into the night.

(SOUND: Heavy frantic footsteps receding down porch stairs)

PETER
(kneeling over Sionna, pressing hands on wound)
Sionna! Hold on! Hold on! I'm here!

SIONNA
(gasping, pale)
Peter...

(MUSIC: Panicked, surging dramatic strings)

PETER
(screaming out door)
Somebody call nine-one-one!

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Winter rain streaks the windows. SIONNA sits in a rocking chair, her left arm in a sling. Her light blue eyes are dull, hollow, staring at the floor. PETER sits on the sofa opposite her, bags under his eyes.

(SOUND: Rain hammering glass, rocking chair creaking slowly)

PETER
Sionna, please talk to me. He's in jail now. He can't hurt us anymore.

SIONNA
(voice flat, without emotion)
I can't... feel myself anymore, Peter. Every time I look at you, I see him coming through that door.

PETER
(tears welling)
It wasn't my fault... I tried to save you.

SIONNA
I know you saved me. But I can't go on like this. I'm moving back to my parents' farm.

(MUSIC: Somber solitary cello melody)

PETER
(whispering)
Don't leave me... you're my whole life.

SIONNA
(looking away)
I'm sorry.

EXT. CEMETERY - DAY

Frost covers green grass. A grey granite tombstone reads: "OLIVIA ANNE OLSEN - 1933-1998 - I HAVE KEPT THE FAITH."

PETER (20s, dark winter coat) kneels, placing a cluster of yellow mums into a metal vase by the headstone.

(SOUND: Bitter winter wind howling through bare tree branches)

PETER
I'm doing my best, Ma. I'm going to finish school. I'm going to make something of myself.

INT. DELI - MILWAUKEE - DAY

Bright, modern deli interior. PETER (30s, polished, sharp suit jacket, mature athletic build) sits eating a Reuben sandwich.

(SOUND: Plates clattering, patrons chatting)

BOB (30s, heavy set, graying hair, worn work jacket) walks past holding a tray, spots Peter, and freezes in shock.

BOB
Holy shit! Is that you, Little Brother?

PETER
(standing, smiling warmly)
Bob?

BOB grabs Peter in a huge bear hug.

(SOUND: Slap on back)

BOB
Look at you, man! Big shoulders, sharp clothes! You look like a boss! Are you back in town?

PETER
Just visiting. I run a business out in Seattle now.

BOB
(shaking head in wonder)
I knew you were smart, bro. I took over Nick's old apartment, workin' at the metal plant now. Nick... he's still up at Green Bay.

PETER
I know. I'm heading there today.

BOB
You got cojones, man. After everything he did... good luck.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTION - VISITING ROOM - DAY

Thick plexiglass separates stainless steel counters. Heavy security guards stand by iron doors. PETER (30s) sits at a stool, picking up a gray telephone receiver.

(SOUND: Heavy steel doors CLANGING shut, muffled echoes)

NICK (30s, frail, scrawny frame, thinning gray hair, pale sallow face, wearing an orange prison jumpsuit) limps in and sits opposite Peter. He picks up his receiver with trembling hands.

NICK
(voice cracked, raspy)
Hi, Bro. Glad you came.

PETER
Hi, Nick.

(SOUND: Fluorescent light humming softly)

NICK
(looking down, tears welling)
I'm so sorry, Peter... about Ma... about Sionna... I was an asshole. A terrible brother.

Peter looks at Nick calmly, without anger or hatred.

(MUSIC: Gentle emotional piano theme entering softly)

PETER
You were an asshole, Nick. You took Ma's life, you almost took Sionna's, and you tried to keep me under your thumb forever.

NICK
(sobbing quietly against the glass)
I know... I know...

PETER
But I'm not here to yell at you. I'm here to tell you that I forgive you.

Nick looks up, stunned, tear tracks shining on his sunken cheeks.

PETER
I forgive you because it sets me free. It releases your control over my life forever. I built my own business in Seattle, Nick. Twelve pizza stores.

NICK
(wiping eyes, smiling faintly)
Twelve stores? You did it, Bro... You really did it. President Olson. I'm proud of you, Peter.

PETER
(nodding softly)
Take care of yourself, Nick. Get better.

The GUARD taps Nick's shoulder. Nick rises slowly, giving a final nod before being led away through the iron door.

(SOUND: Heavy door latch CLICKING shut)

EXT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT - BOARDING GATE - DAY

Crisp autumn sunlight streams through high glass terminal windows. PETER (30s) walks tall toward an airplane jetway, carrying a sleek leather travel bag. His face is serene, relaxed, free.

(SOUND: Airport terminal announcements, jet engine turbine hum outside)

PETER
(V.O.)
The weight of thirty years was gone. I was free to live without fear, guilt, or judgment. Siona planted the seed, and I finally grew into my own man.

GATE AGENT
Boarding pass, please.

PETER hands over his pass. The agent scans it and smiles.

GATE AGENT
Have a great flight, Mr. Olson.

PETER
(smiling to himself)
Thank you.

Peter steps into the jetway toward the bright sky outside.

(MUSIC: Inspiring, uplifting orchestral swell)

FADE OUT.

THE END
